Alright, here's what I've got out.  This is on my 715/75, but I did it
using rbootd, not by writing to disk.

So, an overview of the steps I took:

- installed rbootd-2.0-2 rpm from www.puffingroup.com/hppa-linux/
- edited /etc/rbootd.conf too contain:
8:0:9:87:e4:8f      bootstrap.bin             # Linux

Where 8:0:9:87:e4:8f is my HP's MAC

- copied bootstrap.bin into /export/hp/rbootd/

- started up rbootd with /etc/rc.d/init.d/rbootd start
- on bootup, selected b) for 'boot from specified device', then
'lan.000000-000000.255.255'

The HP then retrieved the boot image, and displayed (I'm typing this, so
there may be typos):

Trying lan.00000-000000.255.255
Boot path initialized.
Attempting to load IPL.

Hard booted.

******************************************

HPPA/Linux Bootstrap Version 0.1
[jason@sathanas Tue Feb 16 16:48:50 CST 1999]
by Jason Eckhardt

CPU: ? (00000000)

Loading kernel...(not yet implemented)

It is now safe to reboot.
Testing mprintf (d) 123 = 123
Testing mprintf (s) jason = jason
Testing mprintf (c) A = A
Testing mprintf (x) 01020304 = 01020304
............................
(and the front LEDs flash on and off with a period of about 10s)

Very cool!

